{"name":"Fish Camp Woods","hex":"#7a9682","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fish","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A typically cold-blooded vertebrate animal that lives in water, moving with the help of fins and breathing with gills; any vertebrate that is not a tetrapod.","Any vertebrate, including tetrapods."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A period of time spent fishing.","An instance of seeking something."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fish"},{"term":"camp","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An outdoor place acting as temporary accommodation in tents or other simple structures.","An organised event, often taking place in tents or temporary accommodation."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To live in a tent or similar temporary accommodation.","To set up a camp."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/camp"},{"term":"woods","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of wood"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dense collection of trees, usually one covering a relatively small area; usually smaller than a forest.","For chemical behavior purposes, trees in full leaf (coniferous or medium-dense deciduous forests)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/woods"}],"entries":[{"title":"Scouting and Guiding in Alberta","blocks":[{"text":"Scouting and Guiding in Alberta has a long history, from the 1900s to the present day, serving thousands of youth in programs that suit the environment in which they live."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a7/Alberta_Council_%28Scouts_Canada%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scouting_and_Guiding_in_Alberta"},{"title":"Fish","blocks":[{"text":"A fish is an aquatic, anamniotic, gill-bearing vertebrate animal with a tough cranium to protect the brain, but lacking limbs with digits. Fish can be grouped into the more basal jawless fish and the more common jawed fish, the latter including all living cartilaginous and bony fish, as well as the extinct placoderms and acanthodians. Tetrapods are descended from fish, so in a break from the long tradition of grouping all fish into a single class (Pisces), modern phylogenetics views fish as a paraphyletic group that includes all vertebrates except tetrapods. To treat fish as a monophyletic group, one would have to classify tetrapods as a clade/subgroup of specialized fish, which would make \"fish\" and \"vertebrates\" synonyms. In English, the plural of \"fish\" is fish when referring to individuals and fishes when referring to species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Balantiocheilos_melanopterus_-_Karlsruhe_Zoo_02_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Balantiocheilos_melanopterus_-_Karlsruhe_Zoo_02_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fish"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/7b/Alberta_District_%28Association_des_Scouts_du_Canada%29.png/250px-Alberta_District_%28Association_des_Scouts_du_Canada%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/7b/Alberta_District_%28Association_des_Scouts_du_Canada%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/7b/Alberta_District_%28Association_des_Scouts_du_Canada%29.png/250px-Alberta_District_%28Association_des_Scouts_du_Canada%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Alberta branch of the Association des Scouts du Canada","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlberta_District_(Association_des_Scouts_du_Canada).png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/8c/Alberta_Council_%28Girl_Guides_of_Canada%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_